8|0@YouShouldKnow^1440966589#cul32u8:wolfgame$:In NYC, a beer is normally $6-$7. $1 per is perfectly normal here. I tip $2 for the first, $1 for everything after, and $2 for buybacks. You run on percentages if you're running a tab. And at open bars, I just give the bartender $10-$20 right out of the gate, depending on how long I think I'm going to be there. Also, coaster over my glass if I'm stepping outside or alternatively wrap a napkin around the top of the bottle, if I'm going that route. While I was working on a book about bar etiquette, just about every bartender that I spoke to knew this rule. You don't do it to secure your drink from roofies, you do it to let the bartender know that you're not a deadbeat, and if the place is crowded to let people know that you're sitting there and will return shortly. If you're concerned about someone dropping something in your drink, finish your drink, or most of it, coaster over the glass, go do your thing, then come back, remove the coaster, and order another drink.
