3|1@YouShouldKnow^1440948238#cuksxq3:adesme$:Ugh... That's perfectly annoying. To me, it's sort of like when someone says, "the bartender served my friend and I." It suggests that you are aware of some common language error, but not really. edit: Not sure why I'm being downvoted, but I can explain the point. The error "Henry and me did..." or "Me and Henry did..." is common and often corrected "Henry and I did..." The problem is that not everyone understands why (Subject-verb-object, "I" is subject and "me" object; "I fucked Henry" vs "Henry likes me") and thus end up thinking that it always should be "[person] and I."
3|0@YouShouldKnow^1440956689#cukxloz:Chaost$:?
1|0@YouShouldKnow^1440969686#cul4ss0:adesme$:Explained in edit.
2|0@YouShouldKnow^1440961103#cul02ae:RsonW$:"I" is the subject, "me" is the object. You wouldn't say "the bartender served I."
3|0@YouShouldKnow^1440963023#cul1416:Throne3d$:Apparently that's called [hypercorrection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypercorrection)! The Wikipedia article has a section on it, under "[Personal pronouns](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypercorrection#Personal_pronouns)". &gt;Jack Lynch, assistant professor of English at Rutgers University, says that correction of "me and you" to "you and I" as subject leads people to "internalize the rule that 'you and I' is somehow more proper, and they end up using it in places where they shouldn't – such as 'he gave it to you and I' when it should be 'he gave it to you and me.'" It also states that "the linguists Rodney Huddleston and Geoffrey K. Pullum" seem to think that people "assume that the case of a pronoun in a coordination must be the same as when it stands alone", and they also seem to suggest that it doesn't work like that. Personally, I think that's stupid. Of *course* you say "She hit they and we", *obviously*, as opposed to "She hit them and us", right? So it's fine to say "She hit you and I", right? No. Though people do say it often enough that, with a descriptivist view, it's somewhat acceptable... But, again, it sounds weird to me, and doesn't mesh in the same way with other usage, so I don't really agree...
